 go  over the border several years ago more  than 700 000 rohingya  muslims fled myanmar for neighboring  bangladesh due to a military crackdown  that has led to accusations of genocide  any hopes that any significant number of  them would be repatriating  you know any time this year this just  seems to completely  dash that because why would they want to  go back to the country that is now  completely ruled  by the same military that drove them out  and killed by some estimates more than  10 000 of them  dave thank you very much for your  insights that was journalist dave  grunibaum talking to us from kuala  lumpur  calm amidst the political storm  people in the city of yangon adjusting  to a new reality  of military rule a state of emergency  has been declared for one year  despite calls for resistance by former  de facto leader aung sang-soo-chi  residents of myanmar's largest city are  we've hesitant for 72 hours but people  won't go on the street in protest  everyone knows the military has guns and  they are used to shooting people  i think people will find another way to  protest  we are very timid and we can't say  anything  i'm afraid of them  many international leaders have  expressed alarm  over the coup the u.s underscored its  support for democracy in myanmar  also known as burma after saying it was  quote  taking note of those who stand with the  people of burma  in this difficult hour i think it's a  message to  all countries in the region and  countries who you know will be asked  to respond or to consider what the  appropriate response will be  in reaction to the events that have  happened over the past couple of days  in the capital napieta hundreds of  lawmakers are confined in government  housing complexes  which are being guarded by soldiers  the military said seizing power was  necessary  after the government failed to act on  claims of election fraud  on facebook the ruling national league  for democracy party appealed to the  military to free those detained and to  honor november's election results  which handed the nld a landslide victory  they were the second democratic  elections in myanmar  after a transition from decades of  military rule  now there are fears the country could  slide back to its old ways  and for more on that let's bring in  felix hydoc he is a researcher for the  asia division at swp the german  institute for international and security  affairs  welcome to the program and thank you for  joining us do you see a path out of this  political crisis in myanmar  at the moment not really to be honest um  the coup  um has created a new status quo  um as you already alluded to  the nld leadership de facto has been  detained  um there's military uh patrolling this  key areas in key cities uh at the moment  i don't see  really a way out i also do not see  the military reacting to international  pressure that will come one way or the  other via the u.n  via the eu the us and other states and  organizations um but  current currently um i do not see a  quick way out no  why is that um i'd like to ask you why  you're skeptical about the results that  international uh pressure  can have in myanmar because you know  over the past years we have seen mixed  results from the international community  on myanmar  there was limited action following the  crackdown against rohingya muslims for  example in the country in  in 2017 is it just because of a lack of  consistency with regard to myanmar  or is it something else  well i think it's not a monocausal in  the sense that there are  i think at least two main factors here  not just one  one being surely there's inconsistent  messaging  there are there are countries like um  like china um who are  more reserved than other countries  but there's also if you look closer  there's the asean the association of  south east asia  where you can already see a split  between countries like  malaysia indonesia and others who are  more  forceful when it comes and more and more  inside the coup and there's other  countries  if already if you can check the  statements by the  thai government but also by the  philippines who kind of treat it as an  internal affair and and kind of shy  away from putting pressure  on myanmar so even on this and that's  the one factor the other factor is  domestic  um there is a long history of the  uh the um burmese military  uh being in one way or the other immune  to international criticism and  and sanctions i mean the country has  basically existed as  the pariah state for decades and in the  in the in the in the eye  in the means of many in the military and  many conservatives in the country  been doing all right with that so it  wouldn't surprise me if this  mentality and this outlook um will also  one way or the other determine the the  the actions and the interests of the  current military leadership in myanmar  felix heideck from the german institute  for international and security affairs  thank you so much  thanks for having me
 

